messages:
Maya: quick sync — PR #452 updates the function-calling guide and adds a structured-output section. I left a few TODOs around optional fields and an example for nullable tool outputs. Can someone pick this up before tomorrow's release?
Sam: Taking a look now. Which examples did you want expanded?
Maya: mainly the 'tool returns optional payload' case + a tiny JS and Python SDK snippet showing parse fallback. Also a short blurb on guardrails when the model spits unparseable JSON.
Eli: I can add the SDK snippets. Quick question: do we want to show the old `response.choices[0].message` parsing pattern or push people to the new `structured_parse()` helper?
Maya: prefer the helper but keep the old pattern as a minimal fallback. People still have legacy code. Add a single-line note that the helper does null-safe parsing and falls back to a string envelope.
Kai: flag on links — PR currently references the internal eval repo link that won't be public. I left a comment with the correct docs.redwood.ai link.
Maya: thanks Kai — leave the corrected URL in your commit. Also we need an example for when the schema expects an array but the model returns a single object (common edge-case). Could show a wrap fallback.
Sam: proposing this tiny example for the 'nullable tool output' case:
```json
{
  "tool_result": null,
  "metadata": {"confidence": 0.0}
}
```
Sam: and then a parsing suggestion: check for null before iterating, or use `ensureArray()` shim in examples.
Eli: here's the JS SDK snippet I'll add (short):
```js
const res = await client.structuredParse(response, schema);
if (res.type === 'ok') {
  const items = Array.isArray(res.value.items) ? res.value.items : [res.value.items].filter(Boolean);
  // continue
} else {
  // fallback: log and return human-readable message
}
```
Eli: note: `structuredParse` returns a discriminated union in our SDKs — mention that in the text and link to the API ref.
Maya: good. Also add a one-paragraph note about guardrails: suggest adding a light-weight post-parse validator that checks required keys and value types and triggers a safe fallback (e.g., ask the model for a corrected JSON or call a simpler model). Keep the code minimal.
Kai: I'll fix the external link and the anchor for the parsing helper. Also added a small sentence about rate-limited retries when asking for corrected JSON (so we don't infinite-loop).
Sam: small nit: grammar in the second paragraph — change "model may return single object" -> "the model may return a single object". I can patch.
Maya: awesome. Could one of you add a single test snippet in the docs repo that runs `assert parse()` on the sample outputs? Not a CI-level test, just an example section so we can catch regressions in later edits.
Eli: will add a tiny pytest snippet and a matching JS jest example.
Maya: perfect. Who's going to merge? I'd rather keep it under review for another hour for the link fix + snippet polish.
Sam: I can merge after Kai's link commit and Eli's SDK snippet commit land.
Kai: pushed the link fix. pinging docs-bot to refresh the preview.
docs-bot: rendered preview updated for PR #452 — changed files: docs/function-calling.md, docs/structured-output.md, examples/js/parse-fallback.js, examples/py/test_parse_nullable.py
Maya: thanks everyone. I'll do a quick pass post-merge to confirm anchors. If anything else pops up, tag me. :thumbsup:
